/// Where a host loop waits.
///
/// Every host loop (a TUI, a daemon's runner, the CLI's own read loop) is written against `await`:
/// park until one of the things it asked for happens, get told which. Under the scheduler the
/// calling process gives up the thread while it waits, so other processes keep running; outside
/// one (a plain `execute`) the thread is held, polling. The loop knows neither.
///
/// Nothing outside this file reads a key with a timeout, sleeps in slices, or remembers when it
/// last looked at the store. If a loop needs any of that, it is asking for an `EventSpec` that does
/// not exist yet, and the place to add it is here.
module Darklang.Stdlib.Host

/// What a process can wait for. `Host.await` takes a list of these and parks the calling
/// process until the first of them happens.
type EventSpec =
  /// The next key press.
  | Key
  /// The package store changed: an op landed here, or was pulled.
  | StoreChanged
  /// Fires once, this many milliseconds from now.
  | Timer of ms: Int64
  /// A process (`Exec.list` shows their ids) finished.
  | ExecDone of id: Uuid
  /// The next line of stdin, without its line ending.
  | StdinLine
  /// The next this-many BYTES of stdin, as text: a body framed by a `Content-Length`. A wait for
  /// stdin that something else beat leaves its read outstanding, and the next stdin wait must
  /// ask for the same thing, since that read's answer is already on its way.
  | StdinBytes of bytes: Int64


/// What the runtime delivers: the first of the requested specs to happen, before `await` has
/// described a store change. `Builtin.hostAwait` returns this; loops see `Event`.
type RawEvent =
  | Key of Stdlib.Cli.Stdin.KeyRead.KeyRead
  | StoreChanged
  | Timer
  | ExecDone of id: Uuid
  | Stdin of text: String
  | StdinClosed


/// What `Host.await` came back with: the first of the requested specs to happen.
type Event =
  /// One read of the keyboard, as the runtime reads it: a key, a paste, or a coalesced burst with
  /// its repeat count. `Stdlib.Cli.UI.Widget.press` narrows it to a `KeyPress` for a widget.
  | Key of Stdlib.Cli.Stdin.KeyRead.KeyRead
  /// What landed: the ops, the names they touched, or `unknown` when it will not say
  /// (`Stdlib.Live.poll` describes it).
  | StoreChanged of Stdlib.Live.Change
  | Timer
  | ExecDone of id: Uuid
  /// What a `StdinLine` or `StdinBytes` read. A stream that went out of step (a byte count that
  /// ends inside a character) raises rather than arriving here.
  | Stdin of text: String
  /// Stdin ended: nothing more will arrive.
  | StdinClosed


/// Say that a loop is about to start, and get the watch its `await`s carry: where its last look at
/// the op log left off. The runtime posts that the store MOVED (a data-version counter, from a timer
/// under the scheduler); which ops landed is Dark's question, answered by `Stdlib.Live.poll` from
/// here. Take it before resolving what the loop will show, so an edit that lands in between is
/// reported by the loop's first wait.
let begin () : Stdlib.Live.Watch =
  Stdlib.Live.watch (SCM.PackageOps.currentBranch ())

/// Park until one of `specs` happens, and say which, with the watch to pass to the next `await`.
///
/// A store change the runtime reports is described against <param watch> before it is handed
/// back; one that turns out to carry no op (the counter moves for a config write too) is not an
/// event, and the wait goes on. A loop holds the watch in its own state: each loop has its own
/// view of what it has already reported.
let await
  (watch: Stdlib.Live.Watch)
  (specs: List<EventSpec>)
  : (Event * Stdlib.Live.Watch) =
  match Builtin.hostAwait specs with
  | Key k -> (Event.Key k, watch)
  | Timer -> (Event.Timer, watch)
  | ExecDone id -> (Event.ExecDone id, watch)
  | Stdin text -> (Event.Stdin text, watch)
  | StdinClosed -> (Event.StdinClosed, watch)
  | StoreChanged ->
    match Stdlib.Live.poll watch with
    | (next, Some change) -> (Event.StoreChanged change, next)
    | (next, None) -> await next specs
